      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/myleus-cf-torquatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Adults of Myleus torquatus are expected to reach about 20–25 cm, with a silvery basic colour, yellow fins and a broad black band bordering the caudal fin (species-specific).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/myleus-cf-torquatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>If the new arrivals really belong to M. torquatus, the adult fish will become about 20–25 cm long. Some specimens are being kept to see what they become.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/myleus-cf-torquatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The smallest babies, about 1.5–2 cm long, are very dark coloured and have a light blaze on the forehead. They float through the water without much movement and imitate a dead leaf. This can make the keeper pretty nervous, but is normal behaviour.</p><p>When the fish are a bit larger, 3–4 cm, they become lighter in basic colour and the blaze vanishes. A diffuse light band over the nape appears, along with an individually differing pattern of spots and stripes. The behaviour differs now, too: the fish become active swimmers and form schools with conspecifics.</p>"
    }
  ],
